MAVSDK's dependency modes
==========================

The Debian build enables MAVSDK_USE_SYSTEM_COMMON_DEPENDENCIES.  The common
libraries (Asio, curl, fmt, liblzma, nlohmann-json, and TinyXML2) therefore
come from packages declared in debian/control.

MAVLink, libevents, PicoSHA2, and libmav are not currently available as
Debian packages.  Upstream release builds fetch their pinned revisions and
link them into libmavsdk.  Consequently, this packaging is suitable for
MAVSDK's upstream binary releases, but its source package is not yet suitable
for the Debian archive: archive builds must not access the network.

Before an archive upload, those four revisions must either be included as
source-package components (with their copyright information) or packaged
separately in Debian.  Once they are available locally, the Debian build can
use SUPERBUILD=OFF with their install prefixes in CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH, and the
git build dependency can be removed.

Python tooling
==============

The traditional superbuild installs the MAVLink generator's Python
requirements into its private build directory with pip.  Debian builds do not
need that bootstrap: python3 and python3-lxml are native build dependencies,
and the generator uses them directly.  The pip behavior remains the default
for builds which do not select MAVSDK_USE_SYSTEM_COMMON_DEPENDENCIES.

Binary packages and supported releases
======================================

The Debian build produces libmavsdk3 for the shared-library runtime and
libmavsdk-dev for headers, the unversioned linker symlink, and CMake and
pkg-config metadata.  Debhelper generates libmavsdk3-dbgsym separately, so
users install debugging symbols only when needed.

Release CI builds these packages for amd64 and arm64 on Debian 12 and 13.
Debian 11 remains on the legacy packaging path while it approaches end of
upstream support; it does not use this dependency and package split.

Installing build dependencies
==============================

Run the dependency installer as root before invoking dpkg-buildpackage:

    sudo ./debian/install-build-deps

For an amd64-to-arm64 cross-build, select arm64 as the host architecture:

    sudo env DEB_HOST_ARCH=arm64 ./debian/install-build-deps
    dpkg-buildpackage --build=binary --no-sign --host-arch arm64

Run these commands from the cpp directory.  dpkg-buildpackage invokes
debian/rules and writes the runtime, development, and debug-symbol packages to
the repository root.  CPack is not part of the Debian packaging path.

The installer contains a narrowly guarded workaround for Debian bug #1111197.
On Debian 13 amd64-to-arm64 cross-builds only, it preinstalls
libpsl-dev:arm64 in a separate transaction where apt passes --force-overwrite
to dpkg.  The two architectures of libpsl-dev contain non-identical copies of
a shared manpage.  All other build dependencies retain the normal overwrite
checks, and native builds and other build/host combinations do not enable the
workaround.  Remove it after the fixed libpsl package is available in Debian
13.

Release version
===============

The package version remains maintainer-controlled in debian/changelog.  For a
tagged release, CI checks that its upstream portion matches the v-prefixed Git
tag before publishing packages; it does not rewrite the changelog.
